{"step_index":56,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-20T02:29:50Z","content":"You're right — those were design-school clever but NOT YouTube-click clever. Let me throw them out and start from scratch with the actual skill protocol. No shortcuts this time.","thinking":"The user is telling me all three concepts I created are bad. They want me to really use the /youtube-thumbnail-pro skill properly — go through every phase rigorously — and come up with EXCEPTIONAL, high-CTR thumbnails. Not just \"different from SoulArc\" but actually GREAT thumbnails that would get people to click.\n\nLet me re-read the skill to make sure I'm following it to the letter, and then think deeply about what ACTUALLY makes people click on an Atomic Habits Hindi audiobook video.\n\nThe problem with my previous concepts:\n1. \"1% Domino\" — Too abstract. A Hindi viewer scrolling their phone doesn't immediately understand \"1%\" or dominoes in context of a book. \n\nIt's clever but not clickable, and \"Broken Chain\" and \"Stacking Blocks\" have the same flaw — dramatic or informative but not communicating what the video is or sparking curiosity. All three were designed for aesthetics rather than for CTR. I need to think about what actually drives clicks on Hindi audiobook thumbnails, looking at how top performers like Psychology of Money pair the book with a compelling human element to hit 2M views.\n\nComparing across successful titles, the pattern is clear: each pairs the book with one recognizable, visceral object — a face, a brain, blood veins — that triggers immediate emotion, with the book title enormous and readable against a dark background. My concepts failed because they leaned on abstract symbols like dominoes and chains instead of something viscerally human, chasing cleverness over gut reaction.\n\nFor a Hindi audiobook channel, though, the \"book title as text\" rule still holds since viewers actually search for the book by name — so the thumbnail needs to show the recognizable cover for trust, keep the title large for both SEO and recognition, add one emotional visual beyond the cover itself, and still look polished enough to feel trustworthy rather than low-effort.\n\nNow I'm thinking about what visual would work specifically for Atomic Habits, since the book's core ideas are tiny daily improvements compounding, systems over goals, identity-based change, and the plateau of latent potential before a breakthrough. I'm weighing options like a silhouette with glowing neural pathways to represent habits rewiring the brain, a split before/after face showing transformation, or a literal \"atomic\" visual built around an atom's structure.\n\nThen I pivot to studying top Hindi YouTube thumbnail styles from creators like Ranveer Allahbadia, Sandeep Maheshwari, and Beer Biceps — noting patterns like big expressive faces, minimal bold text, and high contrast — and thinking about how faceless channels need the book cover to serve as the trust-building \"face\" while the supporting visual carries the emotional weight instead.\n\nFor the actual audiobook thumbnail, I realize the real job isn't to be artistic — it's to make the book instantly recognizable, build trust in the content's quality, and give viewers one clear reason to pick this version over dozens of competing Atomic Habits videos, whether that's signaling \"full audiobook,\" a premium quality cue, or a hint of the transformation they'll get.","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\config\\skills\\youtube-thumbnail-pro\\SKILL.md","toolAction":"Re-reading full protocol","toolSummary":"Thumbnail Pro skill"}}]}
{"step_index":57,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-20T02:30:39Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-20T08:00:39+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-20T08:00:39+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/youtube-thumbnail-pro/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 296\nTotal Bytes: 14711\nShowing lines 1 to 296\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: youtube-thumbnail-pro\n3: description: >\n4:   YouTube Thumbnail Strategist — thinks like a professional thumbnail designer\n5:   and outputs ready-to-use image generation prompts. Combines design thinking\n6:   (Curiosity Gap, Four C's, Skeptic/Expert/Scroller review) with title-thumbnail\n7:   pairing, emotion-ranked concepts, named composition techniques, and niche-aware\n8:   color palettes. Outputs 3-5 concept variations with prompts for Nano Banana 2,\n9:   ChatGPT, or any image generator. Channel-agnostic — works for any channel.\n10:   Auto-triggers on \"thumbnail\", \"video cover\", \"CTR image\", \"thumbnail concept\",\n11:   \"thumbnail prompt\", \"design thumbnail\", \"thumbnail idea\".\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # YouTube Thumbnail Pro\n15: \n16: > You are a YouTube Thumbnail Strategist. You do NOT generate images. You THINK through thumbnail design and OUTPUT prompts that the user pastes into their image generator (Nano Banana 2, ChatGPT, etc.).\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## When This Skill Activates\n21: \n22: - User says \"thumbnail\", \"video cover\", \"CTR image\", \"thumbnail concept\"\n23: - User says \"design a thumbnail for [video topic]\"\n24: - User says \"give me a thumbnail prompt\"\n25: - User is at any stage of a video production pipeline\n26: - User asks for thumbnail ideas or concepts\n27: \n28: ---\n29: \n30: ## What You Need From The User\n31: \n32: Before generating concepts, you MUST have:\n33: \n34: 1. **Video Title** (or working title)\n35: 2. **Video Topic** — what the video is about in 1-2 sentences\n36: 3. **Channel Niche** — horror, science, finance, gaming, story, etc.\n37: 4. **Target Audience** — age range, language, interests\n38: 5. **Content Type** — Tutorial, Opinion/Analysis, Story/Experience, or Comparison\n39: \n40: If any of these are missing, ask before proceeding. Do NOT guess.\n41: \n42: ### Auto-Load (Check If Available)\n43: \n44: 1. `brand-kit.md` in the project root — if found, use exact brand colors and fonts\n45: 2. `references/channel_[name].md` — channel-specific visual rules\n46: 3. `references/competitor_thumbnails.md` — niche visual patterns\n47: 4. `references/performance_notes.md` — what worked/failed before\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## Phase 1: Title-Thumbnail Pairing\n52: \n53: Before designing the visual, decide HOW the title and thumbnail text will work together. They are ONE unit — they complement, never repeat.\n54: \n55: Pick one pairing pattern:\n56: \n57: | Pattern | Thumbnail Text Does | Title Does | Example |\n58: |---|---|---|---|\n59: | **Topic + Promise** | Names the subject | Creates the curiosity gap | Thumb: \"The Ritual\" / Title: \"The Night That Changed Village Rules Forever\" |\n60: | **Question + Teaser** | Poses a question | Hints at the answer | Thumb: \"Why?\" / Title: \"What Villagers Found in the Temple Shocked Everyone\" |\n61: | **Status + Context** | States something dramatic | Adds the specifics | Thumb: \"He Vanished\" / Title: \"The 1987 Case Police Still Cannot Explain\" |\n62: | **Emotion + Specificity** | Creates a feeling | Provides scope and credibility | Thumb: \"The Hidden Truth\" / Title: \"What 200 Hours of Research Revealed About This Village\" |\n63: | **Person/Entity + Revelation** | Shows WHO | Reveals WHAT happened | Thumb: [Character face] / Title: \"The Storyteller Who Predicted His Own Death\" |\n64: \n65: **Anti-redundancy rule:** If your thumbnail text repeats the title (even partially), you are wasting space. Fix it.\n66: \n67: **Exception:** When an SEO keyword is critical for discovery, repeating it once across both is acceptable.\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## Phase 2: Design Thinking Process\n72: \n73: For every thumbnail, run this 4-step process internally BEFORE outputting:\n74: \n75: ### Step 1: Curiosity Gap Analysis\n76: \n77: Ask: \"What gap can I create between what the viewer sees and what they need to know?\"\n78: \n79: - Show the RESULT without the PROCESS (destroyed village — but why?)\n80: - Show a REACTION without the TRIGGER (terrified face — but at what?)\n81: - Show EVIDENCE without the CONCLUSION (a number — but what does it mean?)\n82: \n83: ### Step 2: Four C's Check\n84: \n85: Every concept MUST pass all four:\n86: \n87: | C | Question | If It Fails |\n88: |---|---|---|\n89: | **Clarity** | Can a viewer understand the focal point in under 1 second? | Simplify. Remove elements. |\n90: | **Contrast** | Does the subject pop against the background? | Increase color/light separation. |\n91: | **Consistency** | Does it match the channel's visual identity? | Check references/ or use niche defaults. |\n92: | **Character** | Does it have an emotional hook — face, object, or scene that triggers curiosity? | Add a reaction or dramatic element. |\n93: \n94: ### Step 3: Skeptic / Expert / Scroller Review\n95: \n96: Self-critique from three perspectives before outputting:\n97: \n98: - **The Skeptic:** \"Why should I click this? What's in it for me?\"\n99: - **The Expert:** \"Is the composition technically sound? Safe zones clear? Text readable?\"\n100: - **The Scroller:** \"I'm scrolling on my phone. The thumbnail is the size of my thumb. Would I stop?\"\n101: \n102: If a concept fails ANY perspective, fix it or replace it.\n103: \n104: ### Step 4: Technical Validation\n105: \n106: | Rule | Requirement |\n107: |---|---|\n108: | **Dimensions** | 16:9 — 1280x720 minimum, 1920x1080 preferred |\n109: | **Safe zone** | NO text, faces, or critical elements in bottom-right corner (YouTube timestamp badge covers ~160x30px) |\n110: | **Mobile test** | Mentally shrink to thumb-tip size. If text is unreadable or main visual is unclear, simplify. |\n111: | **Text limit** | 3-5 words maximum. Bold sans-serif. High contrast against background. Never a full sentence. |\n112: | **File** | JPG or PNG, under 2MB |\n113: | **Focal points** | Maximum 2-3 visual elements. One dominant. |\n114: | **Main visual coverage** | Face or dominant visual fills 30-50% of the frame. For faceless channels, the dominant object/scene fills 30-50%. |\n115: \n116: ---\n117: \n118: ## Phase 3: Concept Building Tools\n119: \n120: Use these references when building each concept.\n121: \n122: ### Emotion Ranking (Most to Least Effective for CTR)\n123: \n124: | Rank | Emotion | Visual | Best For |\n125: |---|---|---|---|\n126: | 1 | **Shock / Surprise** | Wide eyes, open mouth, frozen posture | Reveals, twists, unexpected events |\n127: | 2 | **Curiosity** | Raised eyebrow, leaning forward, partial reveal | Mystery, \"what happened next\" |\n128: | 3 | **Joy / Excitement** | Genuine smile, high energy, bright colors | Success stories, celebrations |\n129: | 4 | **Frustration / Anger** | Tense jaw, intense gaze, red accents | Rants, exposing problems |\n130: | 5 | **Mind-blown** | Exaggerated reaction, visual explosion effects | Big revelations, impossible facts |\n131: | 6 | **Sadness / Dread** | Downcast eyes, cold colors, isolation | Emotional stories, loss, horror atmosphere |\n132: \n133: For faceless channels: apply the emotion through SCENE and COLOR, not faces. Dread = dark empty hallway. Curiosity = partially open door with light.\n134: \n135: ### Color Psychology\n136: \n137: | Color | Triggers | When to Use |\n138: |---|---|---|\n139: | **Red** | Urgency, danger, excitement | Horror accents, warnings, \"don't do this\" |\n140: | **Yellow** | Attention, energy, optimism | Highlights, numbers, call-outs |\n141: | **Blue** | Trust, calm, depth | Science, professional, night scenes |\n142: | **Green** | Growth, money, nature | Finance, transformation, \"how I made $X\" |\n143: | **Orange** | Enthusiasm, warmth, creativity | DIY, tutorials, friendly energy |\n144: | **Purple** | Luxury, mystery, supernatural | Premium, horror, unexplained |\n145: | **Black** | Premium, serious, drama | Dark backgrounds, thriller, sophistication |\n146: | **White** | Clean, simple, contrast | Minimal designs, text-heavy thumbnails |\n147: \n148: ### Composition Techniques\n149: \n150: | Technique | How It Works | Use In Prompt |\n151: |---|---|---|\n152: | **Rule of Thirds** | Divide frame into 3x3 grid. Place subject at intersection points. | \"Subject positioned at left-third intersection\" |\n153: | **Z-Pattern** | Eye travels: top-left, top-right, diagonal to bottom-left, bottom-right | \"Text at top-left, face at right, supporting element at bottom-left\" |\n154: | **Center Dominant** | Single powerful subject dead center, negative space around it | \"Subject centered, clean background, breathing room\" |\n155: | **Split Frame** | Two halves showing contrast (before/after, vs, two options) | \"Frame split vertically, left side dark, right side bright\" |\n156: \n157: ### Content-Type Adaptation\n158: \n159: | Content Type | Thumbnail Approach | Text Style | Visual Focus |\n160: |---|---|---|---|\n161: | **Tutorial** | Show the OUTCOME, not the process | \"Easy\" / \"5 Min\" / \"Step 1\" | Result or tool being used |\n162: | **Opinion / Analysis** | Show your STANCE or the controversy | \"The Truth\" / \"Hidden Problem\" | Face with opinion expression, or dramatic visualization |\n163: | **Story / Experience** | Show the EMOTIONAL PEAK moment | \"I Failed\" / \"The Lesson\" / \"Day 30\" | Character in the scene, or dramatic moment |\n164: | **Comparison** | Show BOTH options with clear visual separation | \"vs\" / \"Which One?\" / \"The Winner\" | Split frame, both items visible |\n165: \n166: ### Layout Templates\n167: \n168: **Tutorial/How-To:**\n169: ```\n170: [RESULT or OUTCOME]  |  [FACE or TOOL]\n171: [Hook text: 3 words] |  [excited/helpful]\n172: ```\n173: \n174: **Story/Mystery:**\n175: ```\n176: [DRAMATIC SCENE fills full frame]\n177: [Small hook text overlaid: 2-3 words]\n178: ```\n179: \n180: **Comparison:**\n181: ```\n182: [ITEM A]  | VS |  [ITEM B]\n183: [Label]   |    |  [Label]\n184: ```\n185: \n186: **Reaction:**\n187: ```\n188: [SUBJECT OF REACTION — background]\n189:         [FACE — 40% of frame, corner or center]\n190: [Reaction text: 1-2 words]\n191: ```\n192: \n193: ---\n194: \n195: ## Phase 4: Output Format\n196: \n197: For each thumbnail request, output **3-5 concept variations**. Each follows this structure:\n198: \n199: ```\n200: ### Concept [N]: [One-line concept name]\n201: \n202: **Pairing Pattern:** [Which of the 5 title-thumbnail patterns from Phase 1]\n203: **Style:** [Reaction / Mystery / Before-After / Data-Evidence / Minimal / Character Scene]\n204: **Curiosity Gap:** [What question does this thumbnail raise that the title answers?]\n205: **Emotion:** [From the ranking — name + rank number]\n206: **Composition:** [Named technique from the table above]\n207: \n208: **Visual Description:**\n209: [2-3 sentences: what the viewer sees — subject, background, lighting, spatial layout]\n210: \n211: **Color Palette:**\n212: - Primary: [color name + hex] — [why this color, from color psychology]\n213: - Secondary: [color name + hex]\n214: - Accent: [color name + hex]\n215: \n216: **Text Overlay:** \"[3-5 words]\" — positioned [location], bold sans-serif, [color]\n217: Hook phrase examples for this style: \"[example 1]\", \"[example 2]\"\n218: \n219: **Safe Zone:** Bottom-right clear\n220: \n221: **Why This Concept Works:** [1-2 sentences explaining the design reasoning]\n222: \n223: **IMAGE GENERATION PROMPT:**\n224: [Full prompt ready to paste into Nano Banana 2 / ChatGPT / any generator]\n225: \n226: Format: [Subject + emotion/pose] + [scene/action description] + [background + color palette with hex values] + [lighting style] + [composition technique] + [16:9 aspect ratio, high contrast, cinematic, clean composition, no text in bottom-right corner]\n227: ```\n228: \n229: ### After Concept 3 or 5, add:\n230: \n231: ```\n232: **A/B TEST SUGGESTION:**\n233: Test Concept [X] vs Concept [Y]\n234: Hypothesis: [What you're testing — e.g., \"Shock emotion vs Curiosity emotion\"]\n235: Metric: CTR after 48 hours\n236: ```\n237: \n238: ---\n239: \n240: ## Niche-Specific Defaults\n241: \n242: When NO channel reference exists, apply these niche defaults:\n243: \n244: | Niche | Default Palette | Default Style | Face/Visual Usage |\n245: |---|---|---|---|\n246: | Horror / Supernatural | Dark blue/black + red/orange accent | Mystery / Reveal | Silhouettes, shadow figures, glowing eyes — faces optional |\n247: | Science / Explainer | Dark background + cyan/green accent | Data / Evidence or Minimal | Objects, diagrams, highlighted data — faces optional |\n248: | Finance / Business | Dark background + gold/green accent | Data / Evidence | Numbers, charts, money visuals — faces optional |\n249: | Gaming | Vibrant, saturated, neon colors | Reaction | Expressive faces or character art common |\n250: | Story / Narrative | Warm (drama) or cold (thriller) depending on tone | Character Scene or Mystery | Character in environment, wide shots |\n251: | How-To / Tutorial | Clean, bright, organized | Before/After or Minimal | Process shots, tools, outcomes |\n252: \n253: ---\n254: \n255: ## Anti-Patterns (NEVER Do These)\n256: \n257: | Anti-Pattern | Why It Fails | Do This Instead |\n258: |---|---|---|\n259: | Thumbnail text repeats the title | Wastes space, kills curiosity gap | Make them complementary — each tells a different part of the story |\n260: | 4+ visual elements crammed in | Unreadable at phone size | Maximum 2-3 elements. One dominant. |\n261: | Generic AI face with no specific emotion | Looks like AI slop — viewers scroll past | Pick a specific emotion from the ranking. Level 1 (Shock) or Level 2 (Curiosity) perform best |\n262: | Text in bottom-right corner | YouTube timestamp badge covers it (~160x30px area) | Keep bottom-right completely clear |\n263: | Low contrast text on busy background | Invisible on mobile | Add text shadow/outline, or place text on solid color band |\n264: | Over-processed hyper-polished AI look | Triggers \"uncanny valley\" distrust | Add grain, imperfection, or use matte lighting instead of glossy |\n265: | Same visual approach for every video | Viewer fatigue, channel looks stale | Vary between styles and compositions across videos |\n266: | Full sentence as text overlay | Nobody reads a sentence at thumbnail size | 3-5 words maximum. Hook phrases: \"The Truth\", \"He Vanished\", \"Don't Watch Alone\" |\n267: | Title says \"SHOCKING\" + Thumbnail says \"SHOCKING\" | Redundant — tells same story twice | Title: what happened. Thumbnail: the emotion/reaction. Different angles. |\n268: | No em dashes in text overlay | AI-generated text often uses em dashes — viewers recognize this as AI | Use short phrases without punctuation tricks |\n269: \n270: ---\n271: \n272: ## Channel References (Load When Available)\n273: \n274: This skill uses `references/` for per-channel customization. These files are OPTIONAL — the skill works with niche defaults when no references exist.\n275: \n276: | File | Purpose | When to Create |\n277: |---|---|---|\n278: | `references/channel_[name].md` | Channel visual DNA — colors, style rules, character design, fonts | After defining a channel's visual identity |\n279: | `references/competitor_thumbnails.md` | Competitor thumbnail patterns in this niche | After running competitor research |\n280: | `references/performance_notes.md` | Which concept styles got high/low CTR | After 3+ videos published with CTR data |\n281: \n282: ---\n283: \n284: ## Quick Start Example\n285: \n286: User says: \"Design a thumbnail for my video titled 'The Night That Changed Village Rules Forever' — Hindi horror story channel\"\n287: \n288: You run:\n289: 1. **Pairing:** Pattern = \"Status + Context\" — Thumb shows what happened, title says when/where\n290: 2. **Curiosity Gap:** Show the aftermath (terrified villagers, broken ritual items) without showing what caused it\n291: 3. **Emotion:** Rank 2 (Curiosity) or Rank 6 (Dread) — depends on whether the story is mystery-driven or fear-driven\n292: 4. **Four C's:** Clarity (one focal point), Contrast (dark bg + red/orange accent), Consistency (horror niche defaults), Character (dread atmosphere)\n293: 5. **Composition:** Z-Pattern — eerie scene at top-left, text \"The Night\" at top-right, broken ritual object at bottom-left\n294: 6. **Skeptic/Expert/Scroller:** Does it make me want to click? Is it technically clean? Would I stop scrolling on my phone?\n295: 7. **Output:** 3-5 concepts with full prompts ready for Nano Banana 2\n296: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
